THE FALSE GREEN THAT KILLED THE 8-FRAME WINDOW.  Read the "freed by" stack.

The classifier used to scan the first 8 frames of a stack for the OUTERMOST
operator new / operator delete frame and start assigning blame after it.  A
nested destructor puts a SECOND operator delete on the stack -- the parent
object being deleted, which is what ran our destructor in the first place --
and "outermost within 8" walks straight past the real free site (#3, ours) to
land on the third-party smart pointer at #5.  Verdict: third-party.  A
use-after-free of ours, filed as somebody else's, silently, on a green gate.

Nothing in the fixture set had two operator frames in one stack, so the bug was
invisible to the suite that was supposed to be guarding this file.  The magic
number was the tell: 8 is not a property of anything, it was slack chosen
because the structure had not been worked out.

The anchored skip gets this right by construction.  The plumbing is the
CONTIGUOUS run of frames sharing one origin (tests\framework\Test.cpp) that
contains an operator frame -- #1 and #2.  #3 is a different file, so the run
ends there and #3 gets the blame.  #4 is never reached, so it cannot matter how
many operator frames sit behind it.

Keep both this and third-party-sogou-uaf.log.  They have the SAME first three
frames; only what follows the plumbing differs, and that is exactly the
distinction the classifier is paid to make.

